  "Number": 41,
  "Question": "The least value of the function \\(ax^2+bx+c\\) with \\(a>0\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac ba\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\\)",
    "C": "\\(b^2-4ac\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a}\\)",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Because \\(a>0\\), rewriting the expression as a positive multiple of a square will reveal its least value. For real \\(x\\), completing the square gives\n\\[\nax^2+bx+c\n=a\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)^2+c-\\frac{b^2}{4a}.\n\\]\nThe square is nonnegative, so\n\\[\nax^2+bx+c\\ge c-\\frac{b^2}{4a}\n=\\frac{4ac-b^2}{4a}.\n\\]\nEquality occurs at \\(x=-\\frac{b}{2a}\\), where the square vanishes. All denominators are nonzero because \\(a>0\\). Thus the least value is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4ac-b^2}{4a}}\\), choice D.",
